    "why": {
      "type": "lps-split",
      "settings": {
        "index": "02",
        "eyebrow": "Why learn perfumery",
        "heading": "Perfumery rewards understanding, not memorisation.",
        "text": "<p>Most people begin with enthusiasm and a handful of materials, then meet the same obstacles. A recipe can produce one fragrance; it cannot tell you why it works, or what to change when it doesn’t. These are the questions beginners most often struggle with:</p>",
        "list_style": "numbered",
        "closing_text": "<p>Structured study turns each of these into a skill you can practise. Lessons are sequenced so that every new idea builds on the last — first the materials, then dilution and evaluation, then accords and finally complete compositions — so you always understand the reasoning behind a formula, not just its ingredients.</p>",
        "image_brief": "Close-up of a formulation in progress: a pipette releasing a single drop into an amber bottle on the balance pan, the display reading a weight in grams. Natural side light. Portrait crop.",
        "image_alt": "A glass pipette held above a bench",
        "image_ratio": "4 / 5",
        "image_2_brief": "Macro: three labelled smelling strips fanned in a holder, handwritten material names and times.",
        "image_2_alt": "Labelled amber bottles",
        "media_position": "left",
        "media_width": "equal",
        "sticky_media": true,
        "bleed_mobile": true,
        "caption": "Weighing, diluting and labelling: the everyday habits the course teaches from the start.",
        "tone": "cream",
        "padding_top": 96,
        "padding_bottom": 96,
        "image_stock": "pipette-hand",
        "image_2_stock": "tagged-bottles"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "item_01": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Understanding what materials actually smell like",
            "text": "Many materials smell quite different in dilution, on a strip after an hour, or alongside other ingredients."
          }
        },
        "item_02": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Learning how materials interact",
            "text": "Some materials lift and brighten, others soften, fix or overwhelm. Interactions are rarely obvious from a description."
          }
        },
        "item_03": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Building balanced accords",
            "text": "Knowing which materials to combine — and in what proportion — to create a coherent impression."
          }
        },
        "item_04": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Understanding dilutions and concentrations",
            "text": "Working accurately with weights, percentages and dilutions so results can be repeated and adjusted."
          }
        },
        "item_05": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Developing and evaluating complete formulas",
            "text": "Assessing a composition over time, diagnosing what isn’t working and modifying it with intent."
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "item_01",
